\left(P_{0} - \frac{n^{2}a}{V^{2}}\right)(V-b)$. Rearranging to solve for $P$, we get $P = \frac{n^{2}a}{V^{2} + nb}$. In order to find the constants $a$ and $b$, we need to know the number of moles of xenon. The molar mass of xenon is $131 \mathrm{g/mol}$, so the number of moles is $131 \mathrm{g} / 131 \mathrm{g/mol} = 1 \mathrm{mol}$. The molar volume of an ideal gas at $STP$ is $22.4 \mathrm{L}$, so $b = 22.4 \mathrm{L}$. Now we can plug in the values and solve for $a$. $a = \frac{P_{0}V}{n} = \frac{(1 \mathrm{atm})(22.4 \mathrm{L})}{(1 \mathrm{mol})} = 22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol}$. Now we can plug in the values and solve for $P$. $P = \frac{(1 \mathrm{mol})^{2}(22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol})}{(1.0 \mathrm{dm}^{3})^{2} + (1 \mathrm{mol})(22.4 \mathrm{L})} = \frac{22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol}}{(1 \mathrm{dm}^{3})^{2} + 22.4 \mathrm{L}} = \frac{22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol}}{(1 \mathrm{L})^{3} + 22.4 \mathrm{L}^{2}} = \frac{22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol}}{22.4 \mathrm{L}^{2} + 22.4 \mathrm{L}^{2}} = \frac{22.4 \mathrm{L} \mathrm{atm/mol}}{44.8 \mathrm{L}^{2}} = 0.5 \mathrm{atm}$.
