We have 9 people and 3 groups of 3. This is the same as choosing 3 people out of 9 to form the first group, then choosing 3 out of the remaining 6 for the second group, and finally choosing 3 out of the remaining 3 for the third group. The number of ways to do this is
\begin{align*}
\binom{9}{3} \cdot \binom{6}{3} \cdot \binom{3}{3} & = \frac{9!}{3!6!} \cdot \frac{6!}{3!3!} \cdot \frac{3!}{3!0!}\\
& = \frac{9!}{3!3!3!} \\
& = \frac{9 \cdot 8 \cdot 7}{3 \cdot 2 \cdot 1} \\
