The Clausius-Clapeyron equation relates the boiling point of a substance to the pressure at which it boils. Rearranging it to solve for the boiling point, we have $\Delta T=\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{P_{1}}-\frac{1}{P_{2}}\right)$. The given values are $\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}=40.656 \mathrm{~kJ} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$, $P_{1}=1 \mathrm{~atm}$ (at sea level), and $P_{2}=253 \mathrm{~Torr}=253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}$ (on top of Mt. Everest). The gas constant is $R=8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}$. The boiling point at sea level is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}$, so the change in boiling point is $T_{\text {boiling point }}=373.12 \mathrm{~K}-373.12 \mathrm{~K}=0$. Plugging in the numbers, we get $\Delta T=\frac{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}}\left(\frac{1}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}-\frac{1}{253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}}\right)=\frac{4888 \mathrm{~K}}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}\left(\frac{253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}\right)=\frac{1238 \mathrm{~K}}{1}=1238 \mathrm{~K}$. The boiling point on Mt. Everest is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}+1238 \mathrm{~K}=1311.12 \mathrm{~K}$. However, we are looking for the difference in boiling point, so we can use the equation $\Delta T=\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{P_{1}}-\frac{1}{P_{2}}\right)=\frac{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}}\left(\frac{1}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}-\frac{1}{253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}}\right)=4888 \mathrm{~K}$. To find the new boiling point, we need to subtract the original boiling point: $373.12 \mathrm{~K}-4888 \mathrm{~K}=-3416.88 \mathrm{~K}$. However, this can't be right, because we know that the boiling point can't be less than absolute zero. Since the question asks for the boiling point, we can assume that the answer must be positive. However, the answer is not among the options. We can try to solve for the new boiling point by using the Clausius-Clapeyron equation in a different way. We can rearrange the equation to solve for the new pressure, $P_{2}=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{P_{1}}-\frac{\Delta T}{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}}$, then plug this value back into the equation for the boiling point, $T_{\text {boiling point }}=T_{\text {boiling point }}+T_{\text {Kelvin }}=\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{P_{1}}-\frac{1}{P_{2}}\right)$. Plugging in the numbers, we get $P_{2}=\frac{1}{\frac{1}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}-\frac{1238 \mathrm{~K}}{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}}=0.00061 \mathrm{~atm}$, and $T_{\text {boiling point }}=373.12 \mathrm{~K}+\frac{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}}\left(\frac{1}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}-\frac{1}{0.00061 \mathrm{~atm}}\right)=373.12 \mathrm{~K}+1238 \mathrm{~K}=1311.12 \mathrm{~K}$. Since this answer is still not among the options, we can try a different approach. We can assume that the pressure is proportional to the elevation. The pressure at sea level is 1 atm, and the pressure at the top of Mt. Everest is $253 \mathrm{~Torr}=253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}$. Since the elevation is 8848 m, we can find the elevation per unit pressure as $8848 \mathrm{~m} / 253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}=35,000 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{atm}$. We can then find the pressure at the top of Mt. Everest as $253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}+35,000 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{atm} \times 8848 \mathrm{~m}=253 \times 10^{-4} \mathrm{~atm}+30,999.2 \mathrm{~atm}=30,999.2 \mathrm{~atm}$, which is approximately 31 atm. We can then find the new boiling point as $\Delta T=\frac{\Delta H_{\text {vaporization }}}{R}\left(\frac{1}{P_{1}}-\frac{1}{P_{2}}\right)=\frac{40656 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1}}{8.314 \mathrm{~J} \mathrm{~mol}^{-1} \mathrm{~K}^{-1}}\left(\frac{1}{1 \mathrm{~atm}}-\frac{1}{31 \mathrm{~atm}}\right)=5.0 \mathrm{~K}$. The new boiling point is $373.12 \mathrm{~K}+5.0 \mathrm{~K}=378.12 \mathr